Overview -The automated_image_capture code leverages the pose generation algorithm to map a possible trajectory to capture images. The aim is to automate the trajectory of the robot to move to different locations and capture orientation, pose and image information. This eliminates the need for moving the robot manually and ensures that a sufficient variety of images is captured to annotate and train deep learning models. 
Input expectations - The code expects a list of Poses(from geometry_msgs.msg import Pose) and an integer parameter representing the number of iterations for which the code should run. These iterations are the total number of runs the code will have. Please note that not every run will result in a successful motion. 
Run expectations - The code originally begins with a random pose from the list of poses supplied in the input. In case this pose is reachable by the robot, we start our search for the next pose using the Euclidean distance as the metric. The logic behind this move is to calcualte the distance of the current pose to all the possible poses and navigate next based on the least distance measure. The argument here is that the poses that are closer to each other in distance have a higher chance of being reachable by the robot and increases our chances of getting new orientation information. 
